---
// Project detail, MAP-FIRST — the same record as ../[project].astro, read the
// other way round: the map is the whole pane and everything else floats on it.
//
// THE FIVE QUESTIONS (product brief §5):
//
// 1. THE ONE QUESTION. "What is happening on this ground?" The document reading
//    of this record answers "is this project doing what it said it would" and
//    leads with the numbers that answer it. This screen answers the WHERE
//    version of the same record — what is being worked, where it sits, and what
//    the numbers attached to that ground say — and leads with the ground.
//    They are genuinely two questions, which is why this is a second screen
//    rather than a re-ordering of the first.
//
// 2. WHO LANDS HERE, AND WHO IS HARDEST. A field or program lead who thinks in
//    places: they know the creek, not the project ID, and they arrived to look
//    at it. The HARDEST reader is the member of the public who followed a link
//    from the index — they have no map literacy to fall back on, and a screen
//    whose content is a picture can leave them with nothing. That reader is why
//    the map's legend is real text that never collapses (it names every shape
//    and gives its extent), why the record panel leads with Key facts rather
//    than with the map's own tooling, and why "Hide details" is a mode they opt
//    into rather than the state they land in.
//
// 3. SERIES AND SCALAR — UNCHANGED, AND DELIBERATELY SO. Measures, expenditures,
//    funding and milestones are series and they run in that order, the order the
//    record has; lead organization, program, county and the description are
//    scalars and they are in the index card. Re-sorting them here would mean the
//    same project reads two ways depending on which page you opened, and the
//    brief's rail test does not change because the rail started floating: the
//    panel is still 22rem, and a four-column funding table would still fail in
//    it — which is why funding is in the trailing panel with the other series
//    and not in the record.
//
// 4. WHAT IS IRREVERSIBLE. Nothing on this screen. The one control that changes
//    anything changes what is VISIBLE, is a toggle, is escapable by Escape, and
//    is not persisted — reload and the record is back. Everything else is the
//    same inert Add affordances the document reading carries. So nothing here
//    earns an explanation or a confirm.
//
// 5. WHAT IS CUT. The breadcrumb's third crumb — this page is "Map" under the
//    project rather than repeating the project's name as its own last crumb, so
//    the name in the trail is a LINK back to the document reading rather than a
//    dead word. Also cut: the map's icon on the mode toggle (no glyph in the
//    registry is the honest inverse of `expand`, so the pair would have been one
//    accurate icon and one decorative one), and any second collapse affordance —
//    the panels have no chevrons of their own.
//
// THIS SCREEN RE-ENTERS A NEGOTIATION THE BRIEF RECORDS AS REJECTED, and that is
// the one thing about it worth arguing rather than assuming. §4, "Do not put two
// components in charge of the same space": the project detail page once floated
// the record OVER the map, the map framed its shapes around the panel, and it
// was replaced by a rail because "a rail owns its column outright and neither
// component has to know the other exists."
//
// That verdict is still right for THAT page, and the reason is available there
// and not here: on a page whose main column is a document, a boundary exists —
// there is somewhere else to put the record. On a page whose entire pane is the
// map, there is no arrangement that avoids the negotiation, because there is no
// second column to move anything into. Docking the panels would not remove the
// negotiation either; it would convert it into a RESIZE, and a map that
// re-frames every time a panel opens is worse than one that is partly covered.
//
// What is different is the mechanism. The rejected version had the map guessing
// at a reservation and the panel guessing where the map's bottom edge was — two
// components each holding half of a fact. Here the direction is one way and the
// numbers are measured: the panels are the only boxes that know how much they
// cover, so firma2-project-atlas reads their rendered geometry and hands it to
// the map, which publishes one inset knob and one reflow event and knows nothing
// about what is on top of it. A negotiation is two guesses; this is a
// measurement and a publisher. The brief's lesson — prefer a boundary over a
// negotiation — is why the document reading stays the default and this is the
// alternative, not the replacement.
//
// ROUTE. A child of the project's own path (/prototypes/projects/<slug>/map), so
// the URL says what it is: this project, mapped. Same getStaticPaths and the same
// projectSlug() as the sibling route, so the two can never generate different
// slugs for one project.
//
// ZERO PAGE CSS. The atlas owns the pane's geometry, the panels own their
// surfaces, and every section owns its own markup. This file is the spine and the
// manifest.
import AppLayout from '../../../../layouts/AppLayout.astro';
import Firma2PageHeader from '../../../../components/shared/firma2-page-header.astro';
import Firma2ProjectAtlas from '../../../../components/projects/firma2-project-atlas.astro';
import EsaPill from '@esa/ecology/esa-pill.astro';
import { projects, projectSlug, STAGE_TONE } from '../../../../data/firma2-projects';
import type { Project } from '../../../../data/firma2-projects';
import { getProjectDetail } from '../../../../data/firma2-project-detail';
import { withBase } from '../../../../lib/base';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return projects.map((project) => ({
    params: { project: projectSlug(project) },
    props: { project },
  }));
}

interface Props {
  project: Project;
}

const { project } = Astro.props;
const detail = getProjectDetail(project);
---
<!-- manifest:
  layout: firma2-project-atlas (one full-pane surface; every region floats on it)
  sections:
    - page header          -> firma2-page-header    (slotted into the atlas, so the
                                                     mode can take it off screen)
    - work areas map       -> firma2-project-map    (variant="canvas", the whole pane)
    - record panel         -> firma2-map-panel      (leading edge)
      - rail tabs             -> firma2-project-rail
        - key facts           -> firma2-project-facts
        - contacts            -> firma2-project-contacts
        - comments            -> firma2-project-comments
    - sections panel       -> firma2-map-panel      (trailing edge)
      - performance measures  -> firma2-project-measures
      - expenditures          -> firma2-project-expenditures
      - funding sources       -> firma2-project-funding
      - milestones            -> firma2-project-timeline
    - mode toggle          -> firma2-map-panel      (trailing top; holds esa-button)
  note: the map is not a section of this page, it is the page — so nothing is laid
        out AROUND it and every other region is absolutely placed on it. That is
        what makes "Hide details" free: the panels never took any room, so removing
        them leaves no gap and re-frames nothing.
        The section components and their props are IDENTICAL to the document
        reading at ../[project].astro. One record, two readings, one set of parts.
-->
<AppLayout title={`${project.projectName} — Map — ProjectFirma 2.0`} active="projects">
  <Firma2ProjectAtlas detail={detail}>
    <!-- THE BAND IS SLOTTED, not rendered above the atlas, and firma2-project-atlas'
         own header says why: "Hide details" has to be able to take it off the
         screen, and a component cannot hide a sibling it does not contain.

         THE LAST CRUMB IS "Map", WHICH MAKES THE PROJECT'S NAME A LINK. On the
         document reading the name is the last crumb and takes no href, because
         that page IS the project. Here it is one step up, so the trail does a
         second job for free: it is the way back to the full record, and the
         reader does not have to be told that a second reading exists — the crumb
         they would use to go up is it. -->
    <Firma2PageHeader
      slot="header"
      title={project.projectName}
      editableTitle={{ field: 'projectName', label: 'Project name' }}
      crumbs={[
        { label: 'Projects', href: withBase('/prototypes/projects') },
        {
          label: project.projectName,
          href: withBase(`/prototypes/projects/${projectSlug(project)}`),
        },
        { label: 'Map' },
      ]}
    >
      <!-- The stage pill under the title, the same seat and the same argument as
           the document reading: a pill is not a control and does not belong in
           the actions cluster, and the record is asked about in this order
           anyway — what it is, then where it has got to. The visually-hidden
           "Stage" is load-bearing for the same reason it is there. -->
      <span slot="status" class="visually-hidden">Stage</span>
      <EsaPill
        slot="status"
        label={project.stage}
        variant={STAGE_TONE[project.stage]}
        size="md"
        removable={false}
      />
    </Firma2PageHeader>
  </Firma2ProjectAtlas>
</AppLayout>
